"A landscaping company is dropping off flagstones at a customer’s yard. Each flagstone weighs 75 pounds. If the delivery trucks can haul a total of 2000 pounds, how many trucks will be needed to carry 80 flagstones in one go?","A landscaping company is delivering flagstones to a customer’s yard. Each flagstone weighs 75 pounds. If the delivery trucks can carry a total weight of 2000 pounds, how many trucks will be needed to transport 80 flagstones in one trip?",3,3,4,4,"The problem requires determining the number of trucks needed to carry 80 flagstones, each weighing 75 pounds, given that each truck can haul up to 2000 pounds. 

1. **Total weight of flagstones**:  
   \( 80 \text{ flagstones} \times 75 \text{ pounds/flagstone} = 6000 \text{ pounds} \).

2. **Flagstones per truck (weight constraint)**:  
   Each truck can carry a maximum weight of 2000 pounds.  
   \( \frac{2000 \text{ pounds/truck}}{75 \text{ pounds/flagstone}} \approx 26.67 \text{ flagstones/truck} \).  
   Since partial flagstones are not allowed, each truck can carry **26 flagstones** (as 27 flagstones would exceed the weight limit).

3. **Number of trucks required**:  
   \( \frac{80 \text{ flagstones}}{26 \text{ flagstones/truck}} \approx 3.07 \).  
   Since partial trucks are not possible, this is rounded up to **4 trucks**.

Thus, **4 trucks** are needed to transport all 80 flagstones without exceeding the weight capacity of any truck.

