"John uses a driving service to get to work every day. His job is 30 miles away, so he travels there and back daily. He works 5 days a week for 50 weeks each year. They charge him $2 for every mile, and he also tips his driver an extra $150 each month. How much does he end up paying for the rides each year?",John hires a driving service to get him to work each day. His work is 30 miles away and he has to go there and back each day. He goes to work 5 days a week for 50 weeks a year. He gets charged $2 per mile driven and he also gives his driver a $150 bonus per month. How much does he pay a year for driving?,31800,31800,318,318,"John travels 30 miles each way to work, totaling 60 miles per day. He works 5 days a week, so weekly mileage is 60 miles/day × 5 days = 300 miles. Over 50 weeks annually, this becomes 300 miles/week × 50 weeks = 15,000 miles. The cost for mileage is 15,000 miles × $2/mile = $30,000. 

He tips $150 monthly, totaling $150/month × 12 months = $1,800 annually. 

Adding both costs: $30,000 + $1,800 = $31,800.

