"Mr. Jackson’s got 27 kids in his fourth-grade class. He wants to hand out 2 glue sticks to each one. The glue sticks are sold in packs of 8. How many packs does he need to grab so all the kids get their 2 glue sticks, even if it means he’ll end up with a few extras?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"Mr. Jackson needs to provide 2 glue sticks to each of the 27 students, totaling 54 glue sticks (27 × 2 = 54). Each pack contains 8 glue sticks. Dividing the required 54 by 8 gives 6.75, which means he needs 7 whole packs to have enough. 

#### 7","Okay, let's see. Mr. Jackson has 27 kids in his class, and he needs to give each kid 2 glue sticks. Alright, so first, I should figure out how many glue sticks he needs in total.

If there are 27 kids and each gets 2, that's 27 multiplied by 2. Let me calculate that. 27 times 2 is 54. So he needs 54 glue sticks altogether.

Now, the glue sticks are sold in packs of 8. He needs to figure out how many packs to buy so that he has at least 54 glue sticks. But each pack has 8, so we'll need to divide the total needed by the number per pack to see how many packs are required. 

So, 54 divided by 8. Let me do that division. 8 times 6 is 48, and 54 minus 48 is 6. So that's 6 packs with 48 glue sticks, which is 8*6=48, and there's a remainder of 6. Since he can't buy a fraction of a pack, he needs to round up to the next whole number. So 6 packs give 48, which is not enough. He needs 54, so 6 more left. That means he needs another pack. So 6 plus 1 is 7 packs. 

But wait, checking: 7 packs would be 7*8=56 glue sticks. 56 is more than enough because he only needs 54. So yeah, he needs to buy 7 packs to cover all 27 kids, even though there will be 2 extra glue sticks. 

So the answer should be 7 packs.
